================================================================================================================
VERDICT (PART A)
================================================================================================================
  DE1's numbers stand as arithmetic.  Rebuilt from scratch (analytic phantom-density derivative, no DE1/L352 code), the
  p = 2, x_c0 = 2 edge at M_b = 1e11, z = 2.5 is 135.95 kpc against r_F = 38.59 kpc canonical (m_r = +2.523,
  m_X = +11.887) and 142.44 vs 35.16 kpc alt (m_r = +3.052); p_max = 2.965 / 3.071
  (nu_RAR, x_c0 = 2).  The lead track's code note is right (one nu_mono edge under two labels) and inconsequential:
  below y_s = 2.34 nu_mono IS nu_RAR, and the edges sit at y ~ 0.1.
  What fails is the framework's OWN deep-MOND prediction, not data: the lost interval is y = 0.100-0.008 at
  136-39 kpc (4.7 arcsec) around the most massive flagship galaxy, canonical footing only, at radii
  11.1x r(y = 1).  It is an internal (framework-own-terms) gate: the construction would stop making the flat-a0 prediction
  there and would predict a Newtonian outer disc instead -- itself untested.
